{
  "dom_id": "step-q129487",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ at the point $(1,0,2,5).$",
  "worked_solution": "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})=-2y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})=2z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,0,2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,0,2) & =2(1)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,0,2) & =-2(0)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,0,2) & =2(2)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-5 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,0,2)+(y-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,0,2)+(z-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,0,2) \\\\ w-5 & =2(x-1)+0y+4(z-2) \\\\ w-5 & =2x+4z-10 \\\\ 2x+4z-w-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
